{{smaller block|The most admired of all prophane Prophets, whose predictions have been so much scann’d and cryed up, . . . did ''vaticinate'' here. {{right|''Howell'', Vocall Forrest (ed. 1645), p. 32.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To prophesy; utter prophetically or as a prophet; foretell.

{{smaller block|Instinct, intuition, . . . embosom and express whatsoever the Spirit ''vaticinates''.
{{right|''A. B. Alcott'', Table-Talk, p. 133.}}}}

'''vaticination''' (vā-tis-i-nā′sho̤n), ''n.'' [< L. ''vaticinatio(n-)'', < ''vaticinari'', foretell: see ''vaticinate.''] The act of prophesying; prediction; prophecy.

{{smaller block|For this so clear ''vaticination'' they have no less than twenty-six answers. {{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Works (ed. 1835), II. 333.}}}}

'''vaticinator''' (vā-tis′i-nā-to̤r), ''n.'' [NL., < L. ''vaticinator'', a soothsayer, < ''vaticinare'', foretell: see ''vaticinate.''] One who vaticinates or predicts; a prophet.

{{smaller block|Pythagoras, who travelled far to visit the memphitical ''vaticinators''. {{right|''Urquhart'', tr. of Rabelais, ii. 18.}}}}

'''vaticinatress''' (vā-tis′i-nā-tres), ''n.'' [< ''vaticinator'' + ''-ess.''] A prophetess.

{{smaller block|Their voyage was six days journeying. On the seventh whereof was shown unto them the house of the ''vaticinatress''. {{right|''Urquhart'', tr. of Rabelais, iii. 17.}}}}

'''vaticine†''' (vat′i-sin), ''n.'' [< L. ''vaticinium'', a prophecy, ''vaticinus'', prophetical, < ''vates'', a seer, prophet: see ''vatic.''] A prediction; a vaticination.

